# patch: 012-account-failover-chunked
# format: CHUNKED ONLY (2.1.242+)
# version: authored against 2.1.263
# depends: 002
# description: Mid-session Anthropic account failover and pool-backed token
#              renewal. Two triggers, one behaviour: ask claudiverse for a
#              usable credential.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Without it, a leased session is fixed to the account it got
# at startup. Hit that account's rate limit and the session STOPS — the pool
# reroutes the NEXT process, not this one. Same for token expiry: the server
# keeps its copy fresh, but nothing here re-fetches it.
#
# HUNK 1 — services/compact/precomputedCompact.js, in withRetryGenerator.
#   On a 429, report the limit to claudiverse, take the account it hands back,
#   invalidate the token cache and `continue`. A switch costs one retry instead
#   of a failed turn.
#
#   🔴 POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING: AT THE TOP OF THE CATCH, NOT NEAR THE BOTTOM.
#   v1 of this patch inserted at :116721, just before `let cn = bTn(Ke)`, which
#   is where the 2.1.238 patch sat. It NEVER FIRED. Cause: TWENTY early exits
#   sit between the catch and that point, and one of them claims 429s first —
#     let je = Mr() ? d.fastMode && !Sse() : !1;                  (:116385)
#     if (je && !dR() && Ke instanceof Lt && (Ke.status === 429 || AD(Ke))) {
#         ... d.fastMode = !1; continue;                          (:116555)
#   With FAST MODE ON a rate limit disables fast mode and retries, consuming the
#   error 166 lines before our hook. MEASURED: the sidecar log showed only
#   `leased Anthropic account` and NOT ONE `Switched`, `no account available`,
#   or even the under-threshold line — proof the function was never CALLED, as
#   distinct from returning the wrong answer.
#   ⚠️ I verified the insertion point was correct and never checked whether
#   anything UPSTREAM of it claims a 429. A correct anchor in the wrong position
#   applies cleanly, builds, and does nothing.
#
#   Now inserted immediately after `let ct = await r.onError?.(Ke)` — after the
#   three non-retryable rethrows (O_, Xl, DLP) and after logging and the
#   onError callback, but BEFORE every handler that can continue. The pool gets
#   first refusal; when it cannot help it returns switched:false and every
#   existing handler runs exactly as before. That is also better behaviour on
#   the merits: switching to a healthy account beats degrading fast mode on an
#   exhausted one. This is the 2.1.263 successor to the 2.1.238 site the old
#   004-multi-account patch hooked (Ny6 -> withRetryGenerator, P -> Ke,
#   cq -> Lt, vw3 -> Ako, dIK -> bTn).
#
#   🔑 AWAITED, AND THE SITE ALLOWS IT. The hook lands inside
#   `async function* withRetryGenerator`, on the line after an existing
#   `await r.onError?.(Ke)` — so awaiting costs nothing structurally and buys
#   the thing that matters: the failover no longer BLOCKS THE EVENT LOOP.
#   The first version called a synchronous curl here. That freezes the whole
#   process for up to 10s, and anything waiting on the process — notably MCP
#   stdio — times out. MEASURED on the same mechanism in the upgrade path: the
#   14:40 server push made watcher-2 drop its cv_* tools while its MCP child was
#   still alive, i.e. the parent had simply stopped answering.
#   `await` on a non-thenable is also a no-op, so this stays correct against an
#   older sidecar module that still returns a plain object.
#
#   🔑 Hw() IS LOAD-BEARING. It is the cache invalidator on this version
#   (coreSchemas.js:35005 -> iH() -> Use()). Swapping the env var WITHOUT it
#   changes nothing: the process keeps serving the old token from the memo and
#   the failover reports success while doing nothing. An earlier attempt put the
#   clear inside SidecarClient, where the binding does not exist — behind a
#   `typeof` guard, so it threw nothing, logged nothing, and never ran.
#
#   🔑 `retry` IS HONOURED THE SAME AS `switched` (2026-09-18). A 429 that
#   arrives inside the sidecar's post-switch quiet window is attributed to the
#   account we just LEFT, and the sidecar declines to report it — correctly, or
#   it would cool the account we just switched to. But the first version then
#   returned switched:false, and this hook fell through to upstream, which saw
#   a 429 carrying the OLD account's weekly-limit headers and did what it does
#   for a live limit: opened /rate-limit-options and aborted the turn. With the
#   credential ALREADY on an account at weekly 34%. MEASURED on two seats at
#   once: "Switched to Work" -> "429 420ms after a switch … not reporting" ->
#   modal, until the operator chose Stop and the next request went straight
#   through. The remedy for a stale 429 is to re-issue the request on the
#   credential the switch installed — Hw() + continue — which is exactly the
#   switched path, minus the switch. The sidecar bounds it so a genuinely
#   limited new account still falls through after a few attempts.
#
#   🔴 WHEN NO ACCOUNT IS AVAILABLE WE DO NOTHING AND FALL THROUGH, deliberately.
#   Upstream already owns rate-limit backoff, the resets-at message and
#   auto-continue. Operator: "it should work like claude usually does, it should
#   just stick with the first refreshing one and pause until its available".
#   Substituting our own waiting would replace working behaviour with a worse
#   copy. The account is still cooled SERVER-side so new sessions route around
#   it; this one keeps its token, which stays valid because a rate limit is not
#   an auth failure.
#
# HUNK 2 — main.js, in runOnboarding (ja), the interactive-only path.
#   Registers our pool lease as upstream's SDK OAuth refresh callback. Upstream
#   fires it from recoverFromOAuth401 EXACTLY when there is no local refresh
#   token, which is precisely our state (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN implies
#   refreshToken:null), then installs the result and clears its own caches. So
#   the 401/expiry path needs no logic here at all — only registration.
#   cli/print.js already registers one for print/SDK mode; this covers the REPL.
#
#   ⚠️ GATED ON ACTUALLY LEASING, and that is not cosmetic. The retry loop reads
#   kxe() at precomputedCompact.js:116420: with a callback registered, a refresh
#   that returns the SAME token fails fast instead of retrying N times. Correct
#   for us — the server is the authority, so retrying cannot help — but wrong to
#   impose on a stock session that never leased. Hence the CLAUDIVERSE_TOKEN and
#   CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN guards.
#
# ANCHOR: hunk 1 lands inside `async function* withRetryGenerator(...)` — an
#   ANCHORED name, so even git's @@ context hint is version-stable. The added
#   lines reference Lt / Ako / Mot / Hw, which are minified; all four are
#   verified in scope in that file (118 / 2 / 4 / 18 refs). They sit on added
#   lines, so drift fails at RUNTIME rather than at apply time — accepted
#   because no anchored equivalent exists for them, and the surrounding context
#   lines still fail LOUDLY on structural drift.
#
# ✅ EXERCISED (2026-09-13, both paths, in production):
#   - 429 failover: Personal hit its session limit for real, the hook fired and
#     the seat moved to Work mid-turn. It also exposed the in-flight-429 bug —
#     a stale 429 from the OLD account cooled the NEW one and blocked the whole
#     fleet — which is what FAILOVER_QUIET_MS and the server-side suspect-cap
#     now defend against.
#   - 401 renewal: forced with a deliberately invalid token; the callback fired
#     and the pool re-leased.
#   - switch-back: server push at 14:40 moved a seat from Work to Personal.
#   What is still BELIEVED, not measured: the coalescing of two SIMULTANEOUS
#   429s (failoverInFlight). It needs two concurrent requests limiting in the
#   same tick, which I have not been able to stage.
#
#
# HUNK 3 (added v3) — main.js registers globalThis.__cvClearAuthCache so the
#   sidecar can invalidate the credential memo from OUTSIDE the retry loop. The
#   server-push upgrade path needs it: it swaps the token at an arbitrary moment,
#   and without invalidation the process keeps serving the memoized old one.
#
#   🔴 IMPORTED UNDER AN ALIAS, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE. `Hw` in main.js is a
#   DIFFERENT FUNCTION — `async function Hw(v)` at :6162, exported as `main`.
#   Registering the local one would have re-entered the application entrypoint on
#   every token swap. Two modules, same minified name, both in scope; nothing
#   warns. Caught only by grepping for the definition instead of assuming the
#   name meant what it meant one file over. Same class as the pje collision.
#
#
# ============================================================================
# 2026-09-20 — THE IN-TURN FAILOVER HAD NEVER ACTUALLY COMPLETED
# ============================================================================
# MEASURED, sidecar log, ~20:45:
#     ⚠ Switched to Anthropic account 'Work'
#     429 1277ms after a switch — retrying on the current one (1/5)
#     429 2557ms … 3499ms … 4490ms … 5418ms — (5/5)
#     429 6368ms — retry budget spent, letting upstream handle it
#   Five retries ~1s apart, all 429, against an account at weekly 10%. Those
#   were not stale in-flight requests. The retry loop was re-sending with the
#   OLD credential.
#
# CAUSE: `Hw(); continue;` clears the memo the credential getter reads, but
#   `continue` re-enters the loop holding the credential object `p` it already
#   has. `p = await e()` runs only when the predicate at the top of the try
#   fires — p === null, 401, Kot (401/403), 407+proxy, CJ, TTn (Bedrock/401),
#   NIe (Vertex/401), stale connection, proxy retry — and NO arm is true for a
#   429. So every "switched" failover since this patch was written re-sent the
#   previous token, 429'd again, and fell through to upstream. Only the NEXT
#   query() invocation picked up the new env token, which is exactly why
#   "choose Stop, then it goes through" was the observed pattern from the very
#   first live failover. The mechanism was verified at the hook site and never
#   one line further down the loop.
#
# FIX — three hunks in withRetryGenerator, no minified name on any added line:
#   · the hook sets globalThis.__cvCredentialStale = true before `continue`
#   · the predicate gains `globalThis.__cvCredentialStale === true ||` as an
#     arm, anchored on the context line `p === null ||`
#   · the refetch site clears the flag on the line after
#     `((p = await e()), …)`, so it is consumed exactly where the getter runs
#   The flag is process-global on purpose: concurrent requests that 429 around
#   the same switch each want the new credential, and an extra getter call is
#   cheap. A tree that renames `p` or restructures the predicate fails BOTH
#   anchor hunks loudly.
#
# ⚠️ NOT reproduced end-to-end. That requires an account at a real limit with a
#   switch mid-turn. The 20:45 log is the reproduction of the BUG; the fix is
#   verified to apply, parse, and by reading the loop.

